ieee80211_output: QoS data frames to a QoS peer from the association on,
each TID's sequence numbers continuous through a block-ack agreement,
for a driver that makes the agreements itself (docs/tessronos/
09-network.md 9.6.9); with ADDBA offload, as the release does.

Why: the release sends data frames without the QoS field until a
block-ack agreement for their TID has been made ("We use QoS data frames
for aggregation only"). Those frames take their sequence numbers from
the counter shared with the management frames (the card's firmware
fills them in), and the first QoS data frame after the agreement starts
the TID's own counter at 0, the agreement's starting sequence number
being 0 too. The sequence numbers the access point sees for the
station's data therefore go back to 0 when the agreement starts
(ThinkPad X1 Yoga with a mesh access point, hw11o: 3 to 7 without the
QoS field, then 0, 1, 2 ... on the aggregation queue); after that, the
access point acknowledged every frame, but almost none reached the
network (DHCP's REQUEST unanswered for 30 s, no frame for the station
received). Linux's mac80211 sends every data frame to a QoS peer as a
QoS data frame (ieee80211_build_hdr, the station's WME flag), numbers it
from the TID's counter (ieee80211_tx_h_sequence), sends EAPOL with
priority 7, and iwlwifi starts an agreement at the TID's next number
(iwl_mvm_sta_tx_agg_start: the ssn from tid_data->seq_number). The same
here: the QoS field on every data frame to a QoS peer, EAPOL on TID 7;
the agreement is asked for as before (its start is decided by
TessronOS's layer, tswifi_obsd.c), never for EAPOL.

Not with ADDBA offload (IEEE80211_C_ADDBA_OFFLOAD, iwx): there the TX
command carries neither the station nor the TID (iwx_tx_cmd_gen2 and
_gen3); each of the firmware's queues is bound to one station and one
TID when it is enabled (iwx_tx_queue_cfg_cmd, iwx_enable_txq). A TID
has a queue of its own only once iwx_sta_tx_agg_start has enabled it
for the agreement; until then iwx_tx puts the TID's frames on the first
data queue, which is bound to the management TID (IWX_MGMT_TID). A QoS
data frame of TID 7 or 0 there is one the release never sends, and
iwlwifi never sends one either (on this firmware it gives every TID its
own queue before the TID's first QoS frame, iwl_mvm_sta_alloc_queue_tvqm).
With the QoS field on EAPOL, the AX200 of a Surface Go 2 sent its
4-way's message 2 four times to an access point that resent message 1
each time and ended the association with reason 15 (the 4-way's
timeout) 4 s later; the same access point and handshake work with the
8265 (iwm), whose TX command carries the TID itself. With ADDBA
offload, data frames therefore keep the release's form: no QoS field
until the TID's agreement (and its queue) is there, never for EAPOL.

Applied by tools/openbsd/build.sh to a copy of the file; OpenBSD's tree
is not changed.

